WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.312
Before writing nested queries, inspect the underlying rows.

00:00:03.312 --> 00:00:06.787
Run this simple SELECT and look at the patient IDs and ages.

00:00:06.787 --> 00:00:13.372
Later, when the subquery returns a set of IDs or an average age, you will be able to connect the result

00:00:13.372 --> 00:00:16.932
back to real rows in the teaching dataset.

00:00:16.932 --> 00:00:18.532
This is also a good real-world habit.

00:00:18.532 --> 00:00:20.667
Understand the source tables before debugging a complex query.

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:26.052
Connect this idea to a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up.

00:00:26.052 --> 00:00:32.652
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:32.652 --> 00:00:37.552
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:37.552 --> 00:00:42.737
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:42.737 --> 00:00:47.840
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
